// Topic builders and practice
const topicBuilders=[
 {type:'YOUR JOB',q:'Describe a typical busy day in your job or a workplace you know.',bul:['main responsibilities','what makes it busy','how priorities are organized'],a2:'A busy day in my work usually starts with checking the most important tasks. I often have several things to do, so I look at deadlines first. Then I contact the people I need and organize the work. For example, if a customer has an urgent problem, I deal with that before a less urgent task. This helps me stay organized and avoid forgetting important work.',b2:'On a particularly busy day, I start by distinguishing urgent tasks from important but less time-sensitive ones. I check deadlines, confirm dependencies with colleagues and communicate early if a problem may affect someone else. For example, an urgent client issue would take priority over routine administration. This approach helps me protect deadlines without losing visibility over longer-term work.'},
 {type:'EXPLAIN HOW',q:'Explain how you prepare for an important meeting.',bul:['what you do first','information you prepare','how you make the meeting useful'],a2:'First, I check the purpose of the meeting and who will attend. Then I prepare the important information and write a short list of points to discuss. I also send documents before the meeting if people need to read them. During the meeting, I take notes and confirm the next actions at the end.',b2:'I begin by clarifying the objective and the decisions the meeting needs to produce. I then gather the relevant data, circulate any pre-reading and prepare a focused agenda. During the meeting, I keep the discussion linked to the objective and make sure responsibilities and deadlines are explicit. A short written follow-up prevents misunderstandings afterwards.'},
 {type:'REMOTE WORK',q:'Why might companies want some people to work from home?',bul:['benefits for companies','work suitable for home','possible problems'],a2:'Companies may allow remote work because they can save office space and employees can save travel time. Many computer-based jobs can be done from home. However, communication can be more difficult and some people may feel isolated. I think regular team meetings are important.',b2:'Remote work can reduce office costs and make recruitment more flexible, particularly for roles based mainly on digital tools. It can also improve concentration for some tasks. The main risks are weaker informal communication, isolation and uneven access to information. Clear expectations and regular collaboration routines can reduce those problems.'},
 {type:'LEADERSHIP',q:'What makes a good manager?',bul:['important qualities','supporting a team','example'],a2:'A good manager listens to people and explains things clearly. They should help the team when there is a problem and give useful feedback. For example, if an employee makes a mistake, a good manager explains how to improve instead of only criticizing. This helps people learn and feel confident.',b2:'A strong manager combines clarity with trust. They set priorities, explain why decisions are being made and create enough psychological safety for people to raise problems early. For example, constructive feedback should identify the issue while also giving the employee a practical route to improve. That approach strengthens both performance and engagement.'},
 {type:'FIRST JOBS',q:'What can people learn from their first job?',bul:['professional habits','communication','future value'],a2:'People can learn many things in a first job. They learn to arrive on time, organize tasks and work with different people. They also learn how to speak to customers or colleagues professionally. These habits are useful later because they help people understand how a workplace works.',b2:'A first job often teaches professional habits that are difficult to learn in theory, such as reliability, prioritization and accountability. It also develops communication because new employees must adapt to colleagues, customers and managers. Even if the role is unrelated to a later career, those transferable behaviours create a strong professional foundation.'},
 {type:'CUSTOMER SERVICE',q:'How should an employee handle an unhappy customer?',bul:['first response','finding a solution','when to involve a manager'],a2:'First, the employee should listen carefully and stay calm. Then they should apologize if there is a problem and explain what they can do. If the problem is difficult or the customer is still unhappy, they should ask a manager for help. The customer needs to feel that the problem is taken seriously.',b2:'The first priority is to listen without becoming defensive and clarify exactly what has gone wrong. The employee should acknowledge the impact, explain the available options and agree on a realistic next step. Escalation is appropriate when the issue exceeds the employee’s authority, involves significant risk or remains unresolved despite reasonable options.'},
 {type:'TEAMWORK',q:'When is teamwork better than working independently?',bul:['a situation where teamwork helps','one challenge','how to make teamwork effective'],a2:'Teamwork is useful when a task needs different skills or several people must coordinate. One difficulty is that communication can take time. To make teamwork effective, people need clear roles and deadlines. For example, on a large project, one person can organize the schedule while others focus on different tasks.',b2:'Teamwork is particularly valuable when a task depends on complementary expertise or rapid coordination across functions. The main risk is unclear ownership, which can slow decisions or duplicate work. Clear roles, shared deadlines and concise communication routines make collaboration more efficient. For instance, a cross-functional launch works best when each team owns a defined deliverable but shares progress regularly.'},
 {type:'CHANGE',q:'How should a company introduce a new system or process?',bul:['what to explain first','how to prepare employees','how to check whether the change works'],a2:'First, the company should explain why the new system is needed. Then employees need clear training and time to practise. Managers should also ask for feedback after the launch. For example, a short trial period can help the company find problems before the system becomes permanent.',b2:'A successful change should begin with a clear explanation of the problem the new process is intended to solve. Employees then need practical training, realistic examples and accessible support during the transition. I would also recommend a pilot period with measurable feedback, because adoption problems are easier to correct before a full rollout.'},
 {type:'TECHNOLOGY / AI',q:'How can technology or AI help employees at work?',bul:['one useful application','one benefit','one risk or limitation'],a2:'Technology can help employees save time on repetitive tasks. For example, AI can summarize information or help organize documents. This can give people more time for important work. However, employees still need to check the information because technology can make mistakes.',b2:'Technology and AI can improve productivity by handling repetitive analysis, drafting or information retrieval, which allows employees to focus on judgement and relationship-based work. The benefit is speed, but the main limitation is reliability: outputs still need human verification, particularly when decisions affect customers, compliance or safety.'},
 {type:'DEADLINE / PROBLEM-SOLVING',q:'What should a team do when an important deadline may be missed?',bul:['what to check first','who to inform','how to decide the next actions'],a2:'First, the team should check what work is still unfinished and why there is a delay. Then they should inform the manager or client early. After that, they can decide which tasks are most important and whether more help is needed. It is better to explain the problem early than wait until the deadline.',b2:'The team should first identify the critical unfinished tasks and the cause of the delay. Stakeholders need an early, factual update rather than a last-minute surprise. The team can then protect the highest-priority deliverables, reassign resources if necessary and agree a realistic recovery plan with clear owners and dates.'}
];
function topicLanguageFor(type){const common={
 'YOUR JOB':'Useful language: responsibilities · priorities · deadlines · deal with · coordinate',
 'EXPLAIN HOW':'Useful language: first · then · after that · make sure · the final step is',
 'REMOTE WORK':'Useful language: flexibility · commute · productivity · communication · isolation',
 'LEADERSHIP':'Useful language: give feedback · set priorities · delegate · support · trust',
 'FIRST JOBS':'Useful language: reliability · professional habits · transferable skills · communicate · learn from',
 'CUSTOMER SERVICE':'Useful language: listen carefully · acknowledge · resolve an issue · option · escalate',
 'TEAMWORK':'Useful language: collaborate · clear roles · shared objective · coordinate · responsibility',
 'CHANGE':'Useful language: explain the reason · training · adoption · feedback · trial period',
 'TECHNOLOGY / AI':'Useful language: automate · repetitive task · efficiency · verify · limitation',
 'DEADLINE / PROBLEM-SOLVING':'Useful language: deadline · priority · delay · stakeholder · recovery plan'
};return common[type]||'Useful language: reason · example · result · however · overall';}
